# Line endings are part of the product here, not a preference.
#
# This repository has no .gitattributes and Git for Windows defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true, so a Windows clone gets every shell script back with CRLF
# terminators. Git stores LF, so CI on Linux never sees it and every job stays
# green -- the failure is invisible from the place that checks.
#
# It matters because install.sh COPIES these hooks into a user's repository.
# The hooks then run under whatever bash that user's environment provides, and
# a CRLF script under a Linux bash dies on its first line:
#
#   .claude/hooks/gate-check.sh: line 2: $'\r': command not found
#
# A hook that dies on line 2 does not block anything. It is the exact failure
# this pipeline exists to remove -- an enforcement layer that is not running,
# reporting nothing, indistinguishable from one that is working -- shipped by
# the install step itself. Install on Windows, run on WSL or in a container,
# and the gate is gone.
#
# So: shell is pinned to LF everywhere, regardless of platform or local config.
* text=auto

*.sh text eol=lf
*.jq text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# manifest.conf is SOURCED (`. "$M"`), so a CR rides along on the last value of
# every assignment: TIER_ID ends with a carriage return and compares equal to nothing.
*.conf text eol=lf

# A workflow file is shell too, one indent level down. The runner extracts each
# `run:` block and feeds it to bash, so CRLF here puts a CR on the end of every
# command line in every job -- on the Linux runner, where it is fatal.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Hook and script TEMPLATES are shell too, whatever they are named, and these
# are the files that land in a user's repository.
templates/common/hooks/** text eol=lf
templates/common/scripts/** text eol=lf

# Coverage fixtures are captured bytes from real test runners, and the point of
# them is that they are what a runner actually printed. Re-terminating those
# lines edits the evidence: every parser in coverage-gate.sh is anchored with
# `[[:space:]]` and `$`, and a stray CR is whitespace that was not in the
# original output. A fixture normalised by a checkout is no longer a capture.
test/fixtures/** -text
